  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rounds of showers and thunderstorms through this\nevening.\n\nA weak mid-level shortwave and warm frontal segment will continue\nto move across the region through this afternoon. An initial \nround of widespread showers and isolated embedded thunder will \nmove across the region from west to east through at least early \nafternoon with these feature. A secondary round of showers with \nembedded thunderstorms will develop this afternoon and evening \nwith a cold frontal passage. Instability will initially be muted\nearlier in the day from the first round of showers and cloud \ncover, however with some break in cloud cover in the early \nafternoon should build weak to moderate instability. Marginal \nshear profiles with 0-6 km bulk shear of 30 to 40 knots could \ndevelop an isolated strong to severe thunderstorms. Primary \nhazards are damaging winds and large hail. The greatest risk for\nstrong to severe storms will lie across western New York. An \nisolated tornado can't be ruled out across the western Southern \nTier. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Trending warmer this weekend and into next week.\n\nA mid to upper level ridge will begin to build across the Great \nLakes this weekend before shifting east into the Northeastern \nportions of the CONUS through next week. As such, temperatures will \ngradually increase by the end of the weekend and into early next \nweek. Heat and humidity will then increase further through the \nmiddle of next week, which may result in the potential for heat-\nrelated headlines as the risk for dangerous heat indices increase.\n\nThis all being said, heat will greatly depend on the placement of \nthe ridge axis. If the axis lies to the west of the area, there is a \nthreat for \"ridge runners\" next week which not only would decrease \nthe chance for heat headlines but also introduce daily chances for \nrain.",
